parts.text:
Now I have the full picture:

1. **main.js is orphaned** — no HTML page loads it. So its fake `handleFormSubmit` never runs. My earlier assessment needs correction.
2. **contact.html** has a proper Formspree fetch handler in its inline script. The form should actually work.
3. **index.html** has no form at all.

Wait — but is there really an issue? Let me think again about contact.html's form handling. It does `fetch(form.action, { method: 'POST', body: formData, headers: {'Accept': 'application/json'} })`. That's the correct Formspree AJAX pattern. 

Hmm, but wait — there's one potential issue: `formData` includes the newsletter checkbox, fine. Formspree accepts it. That looks fine.

So what's the "form submission issue" to fix? Let me reconsider. In my summary I said: "main.js:335 intercepts ALL form submits with a fake setTimeout 'success' simulation — it never actually POSTs to Formspree."

But now I've discovered main.js isn't loaded by any page. So:
- The fix could be multi-part:
  1. main.js is dead code — either remove it or wire it up properly.
  2. If any future form gets added and main.js loaded, the fake handler would break it.

The user asked to "fix the form submission issue". The best fix:

1. Fix `main.js`'s `handleFormSubmit` to do a real Formspree submission (fetch with Accept: application/json) instead of the fake simulation — making main.js safe to use.
2. Also, since contact.html duplicates all this logic, maybe keep it as-is since it works.

Actually wait. Let me re-verify that main.js truly isn't referenced. Let me grep more thoroughly for "main.js" across all files including sitemap.html.

Earlier grep with include=*.html pattern `main\.js|<script` returned matches only from contact.html and index.html script tags — no src="main.js". But let me be thorough and check sitemap.html explicitly too... Actually the grep covered all *.html files in the directory (not recursive subdirs needed since all HTML at root). Sitemap.html had no matches for either pattern, meaning no scripts at all there.

So the real issues:
1. **main.js dead code**: not included anywhere; if it were included on contact.html, its `setupEventListeners` would attach the fake handler to the form IN ADDITION to the inline one (both listeners fire; both call preventDefault; the fetch would still run from inline script though). Order matters but both run.
2. The fake handler in main.js shows "Sent Successfully!" without sending anything.

Given the README documents main.js as part of the site structure, the cleanest fix honoring the user's request ("fix the form submission issue"):

Option A: Make main.js's handleFormSubmit do a real Formspree POST (respecting the form's action attribute), so any page loading main.js has working forms. This fixes the root issue in the shared script.

Option B: Delete main.js entirely since it's unused.

I think the right move is Option A — fix the shared script so forms genuinely submit via Formspree AJAX, with success/error feedback. This makes the codebase consistent and fixes the issue I flagged. Deleting might surprise the user since README references it.

Also worth noting to the user: main.js currently isn't even loaded by any page — contact.html handles its own submissions correctly via its inline script. I should mention this correction honestly.

Let me implement the fix in main.js:

```js
async handleFormSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();

    const form = e.target;
    const submitBtn = form.querySelector('button[type="submit"]');
    const originalHTML = submitBtn.innerHTML;
    submitBtn.innerHTML = 'Sending...';
    submitBtn.disabled = true;

    try {
        const response = await fetch(form.action, {
            method: 'POST',
            body: new FormData(form),
            headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json' }
        });

        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Form submission failed');

        Utils.showNotification('Message sent successfully!', 'success');
        form.reset();
        submitBtn.innerHTML = 'Sent Successfully!';
        submitBtn.style.background = '#10b981';
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error:', error);
        Utils.showNotification('Error sending message. Please try again.', 'error');
        submitBtn.innerHTML = originalHTML;
    } finally {
        setTimeout(() => {
            submitBtn.innerHTML = originalHTML;
            submitBtn.disabled = false;
            submitBtn.style.background = '';
        }, 2000);
    }
}
```

Hmm, careful: if form.action is empty or "#", fallback. Forms here have action set. Keep it simple.

Also handle success/error display similar to existing style. Let me write it cleanly, matching codebase style (they use .then/.catch style in contact.html, but async/await is fine and cleaner; the codebase uses modern JS classes).

Actually let me match the existing promise-chain style used in contact.html for consistency. Either way works. I'll use async/await as it's cleaner, but consistency argues for then/catch. The main.js class methods are plain ES6+; async/await fits fine.

One more consideration: should I also add a guard so forms WITHOUT an action attribute don't attempt fetch? E.g., `if (!form.action || form.action === location.href)`. Minor. I'll handle gracefully.

Let me make the edit.

